WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:01.540
- [Host] Thanks to Mack Weldon

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.180
for keeping Legal Eagle in the air

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.240
and helping me look fly.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.393
What it's like doing a
virtual depo right now.

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:08.710
(host laughs)

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.920
If you're in Florida,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.820
this is what you look like

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:11.980
because you're not wearing a shirt.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.520
Because apparently
they're not wearing shirts

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.250
in Florida, because
(fan cheers)

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.764
Florida.

00:00:16.764 --> 00:00:21.764
(calming jazz music)
(raindrops pitter-pattering)

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:28.588
(distant sirens ring)

00:00:31.581 --> 00:00:34.331
(window swishes)

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.260
(beverage trickles)

00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:50.070
(fire crackles)

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.240
Oh, I didn't see you there,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.630
and set up a tri-pod and a camera

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.610
and a whole bunch of lights.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.170
Well, since you're here,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.720
and we're all locked inside in

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.650
this never-ending Chekhov's winter,

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.560
this prison of our own homes,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.230
this panopticon of social media

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.240
and lockdown orders,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.860
I guess we might as well do a meme review.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.700
Since everyone loves
lawyers and Coronavirus,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.590
might as well combine

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.760
everyone's two favorite things together.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.719
Yeah, let's do a
legal-Coronavirus meme review.

00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:27.830
(drums beat)

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.250
Meme Review!

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.950
Meme Review trademark of PewDiePie.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.921
All rights reserved.

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:33.183
(drums beat)

00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:34.480
As a criminal lawyer,

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.760
what's the biggest issue you're dealing

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.850
with in 2020?

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.450
Trying to figure out how to correctly run

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.673
the vacuum cleaner and the dishwasher.

00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:42.430
(host laughs) Oh man,

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.995
it's been so interesting seeing

00:01:44.995 --> 00:01:47.841
how the legal profession functions

00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:51.210
when we're all working
from home these days.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.810
It's been really interesting seeing

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.070
that a lot of the stuff

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.100
that makes lawyers expensive,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.240
like going into court and waiting there

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.730
for hours just to have a minor hearing,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.377
can actually be accomplished

00:02:02.377 --> 00:02:04.240
over video conferencing software.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.540
Yeah, trying to work the vacuum cleaner

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.650
and the dishwasher while
sometimes appearing

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.550
in court can be very difficult.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.500
And apparently in Florida
they are appearing

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.240
on video conferences from
home without shirts on.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.310
So all of you lawyers out there,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.270
please put a shirt on.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.210
If you're showing up to court,

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.871
put on a tie even if
it's in your own home.

00:02:24.871 --> 00:02:26.930
(drums beat)

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.170
No, you can't approach the bench

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.241
if you have a cold.

00:02:29.241 --> 00:02:31.170
Oh, big mistake!

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.003
Big mistake.

00:02:32.003 --> 00:02:34.103
He approached the judge without asking

00:02:34.103 --> 00:02:35.730
for permission first.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.930
You know what happens when you approach

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:38.810
the judge without asking

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.290
for permission entering the well,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.320
but this cartoonist screwed that up

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.850
because you wouldn't ask for permission

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.228
if you were already there.

00:02:45.228 --> 00:02:46.916
Think about that.

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.030
(drums beat)

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.700
If you are experiencing
the following symptoms:

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:51.533
headache, body aches,

00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:52.540
shortness of breath, fever,

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.680
developed aches and pains,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:54.540
difficulty sleeping,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.860
easily frustrated,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.750
can't communicate properly,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.940
feeling dizzy, this in
not the Coronavirus,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.245
these are the effects
of being an attorney.

00:03:00.245 --> 00:03:03.040
(host laughs)

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.030
Oh boy that hits close to home.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.760
It can be tough being a lawyer out there.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.647
And look, I know it's tough

00:03:08.647 --> 00:03:10.750
for everyone these days,

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.740
but yeah, lawyers deal with this stuff

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.010
on a regular basis.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.740
'Cause we work really, really long hours.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.850
We'll work 10, 12 hours a day,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.020
seven days a week often

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.410
if we're preparing for trial.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.460
Often people confuse being a jerk

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.490
with being a disposition.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.100
No, it's actually just a consequence

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:27.933
of becoming a lawyer.

00:03:27.933 --> 00:03:29.422
You become a jerk,

00:03:29.422 --> 00:03:30.810
and you get all the aches and pains

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.823
that go with it.

00:03:31.823 --> 00:03:33.489
So, yeah.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.038
I feel that.

00:03:35.038 --> 00:03:35.970
(drums beat)

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.524
What it's like doing a
virtual depo right now.

00:03:38.524 --> 00:03:41.114
(host laughs)

00:03:41.114 --> 00:03:42.140
Uh yeah.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.370
As you may know if you watch this channel,

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.550
a deposition is questioning
a witness under oath,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.630
and you'll have a
stenographer there taking

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.150
down the words that are being spoken.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.880
Well you can't do that now because you're

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:53.713
within six feet,

00:03:53.713 --> 00:03:54.640
you're within the same room.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.920
You don't want to spread the germs.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.230
So, what lawyers have taken

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.660
to doing is doing a virtual depo.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.600
You are having a video conference,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.700
and the witness is still under oath.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.837
You have the stenographer call in

00:04:07.837 --> 00:04:09.790
to the virtual depo,

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.050
and maybe they're recording it

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.010
for future posterity,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.360
which is something they should do anyway,

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.390
and it drives me insane
that they don't record

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.430
these things often.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:17.360
It should be recorded.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:18.410
You should be able to refer back to it

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.490
without having to refer to the transcript

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.323
from the stenographer.

00:04:21.323 --> 00:04:22.930
But anyway, if you're in Florida,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:23.870
this is what you look like

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.030
because you're not wearing a shirt.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.550
Because apparently
they're not wearing shirts

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.300
in Florida because
(fan cheers)

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.527
Florida.

00:04:29.527 --> 00:04:31.120
(drums beat)

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.201
Divorce lawyers in April

00:04:32.201 --> 00:04:34.725
after two-week Coronavirus quarantine.

00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:36.150
(host laughs)

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.070
You know consider yourself lucky

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.334
if you have a significant other

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:39.900
to spend the quarantine with,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.889
and you come out stronger at the end

00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:43.271
of the quarantine.

00:04:43.271 --> 00:04:45.270
(drums beat)

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.900
Government orders non-essential workers

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:47.733
to work from home.

00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:50.143
Lawyers somehow are essential workers.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.850
Well look,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.520
especially you have to
imagine district attorneys

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:56.990
and public defenders,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.880
certainly essential workers,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.520
and as I mentioned in another video,

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.100
the prisons are being hit really,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.500
really hard by Coronavirus,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.860
because you can imagine everyone is forced

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.280
to be in confinement,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.074
and we're hearing numbers of like 80,

00:05:10.074 --> 00:05:12.320
90 and 100% infection
rates in some prisons.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.830
It's a really, really
bad time for lawyers,

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.040
for prison guards, for judges,

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.090
for district attorneys, public defenders.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.550
It's not really a laughing matter.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.890
I mean, I feel it's tough for everyone.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.723
But you know,

00:05:26.723 --> 00:05:27.860
life has to go on.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.185
The wheels of the judicial
system have to go on.

00:05:30.185 --> 00:05:32.661
(drums beat)

00:05:32.661 --> 00:05:34.900
COVID-19 turning lawyers into cavemen.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.262
Last day before--

00:05:36.262 --> 00:05:37.700
Oh poor Matthew Perry.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.855
Remember Matthew Perry?

00:05:39.855 --> 00:05:41.320
He was a good-looking guy.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.218
Yeah, I am trying to workout,

00:05:45.218 --> 00:05:48.095
but I think I'm gonna be leaving lockdown

00:05:48.095 --> 00:05:51.270
with a few extra Coronavirus pounds.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.796
It's just hard not leaving the house,

00:05:53.796 --> 00:05:56.700
going on maybe one walk.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.613
I think I went outside a couple days ago.

00:05:58.613 --> 00:05:59.780
That was good time.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.300
I feel like we're all
at home doing nothing

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.780
but sitting around and drinking Mr. Pibb

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.000
and Red Vines and talking

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.691
about friends alum Matthew Perry.

00:06:07.691 --> 00:06:10.390
(drums beat)

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.490
You know this would be
more fun with a friend.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:14.323
I got an idea.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.610
Hey, are you doing anything right now?

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.080
Yeah somebody set up a tripod

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.890
and a camera in my family room.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.540
You wanna react to some legal memes?

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.240
Cool. I'll send you the Zoom link.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.510
Okay, is this working?

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.850
- Hello.
- Uh you're frozen.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.528
Yeah why don't you try and log in again?

00:06:33.528 --> 00:06:35.090
Yeah, no, try it again.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:36.580
No, I can't hear you.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:37.576
Can you hear me?

00:06:37.576 --> 00:06:39.417
(bell dings)
All right.

00:06:39.417 --> 00:06:41.420
(drums beat)

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.020
- All right, day seven of quarantine,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.260
attorneys let's argue,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:45.660
I wanna feel something.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.670
Oh man, I really feel that.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.730
Do you feel that?
- I feel this deep.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:51.390
I feel this deep for two reasons.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.240
Firstly, my housemates are lawyers,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.240
and so we get our
arguing to another level.

00:06:59.475 --> 00:07:02.020
There is no playing around when it comes

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.900
to who hasn't done the dishes

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.943
'cause we are ready to moot.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.255
- Do people ever talk to you and say,

00:07:08.255 --> 00:07:10.380
I'm really good at arguing,

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.067
therefore I should go to law school?

00:07:12.067 --> 00:07:13.960
- People don't say it to me,

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.410
because I don't think I am.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.243
In real life I think I am

00:07:16.243 --> 00:07:18.300
the most non-confrontational person,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.260
so I actually don't argue a lot.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.172
But you know when you kind of been in,

00:07:22.172 --> 00:07:23.490
not an argument,

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.240
but when you've kind of
fallen out with a friend.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.640
And then you stand in the
shower later listening

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:28.960
to emotional Adele music,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.050
and you think of all the
things you could've said

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.123
in that moment.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.180
Maybe this is not relatable
with the Adele point,

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.890
but kind of like when you come away

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.200
from an argument or a confrontation,

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.530
and you think of all the arguments

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:41.630
you could've made,

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.480
and that's when I knew
I'd be a good lawyer

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:44.313
'cause I was like,

00:07:44.313 --> 00:07:45.540
you see, lawyers don't have

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.100
to be super confrontational in person

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:47.933
'cause they can do all

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:49.140
their preparation beforehand

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.260
and turn up with all of their arguments

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.988
in their pristine, golden, shiny form.

00:07:53.988 --> 00:07:56.290
(drums beat)

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:57.960
Family, religion, friendship,

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.650
these are the three demons you must slay

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.002
if you want to succeed in big law.

00:08:01.002 --> 00:08:01.835
(Eve laughs)
- Oh no!

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.128
It's true.
- Yeah, it's true.

00:08:06.128 --> 00:08:07.203
I agree.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.880
- How would you say your life has changed

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.540
under the Damocles of the Coronavirus

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.883
and being forced to lockdown?

00:08:14.883 --> 00:08:17.150
Are you less social or more social

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.400
than working normal hours at the firm?

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.371
- That is a really hard one

00:08:21.371 --> 00:08:23.090
because I train with a lot

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.830
of other trainee lawyers my own age,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.830
and we often go out for coffee together.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:28.880
And we eat lunch together.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:30.030
And I really like that.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.510
And now I'm so stir-crazy 'cause

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:33.450
I don't have someone

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:36.110
to just break out and talk to.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.827
So I'm probably more anti-social.

00:08:37.827 --> 00:08:39.870
(host laughs)
But I'm definitely outside

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:40.703
a lot more,

00:08:40.703 --> 00:08:41.570
which sounds kind of depressing.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.361
But I often think it's very easy

00:08:43.361 --> 00:08:45.190
when you work at a law firm

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.250
that you will just be inside all day.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:48.970
Unless you make the active effort

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:52.420
to go and get coffee or
go and eat lunch out,

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.010
you won't leave.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:54.843
You won't leave.

00:08:54.843 --> 00:08:58.588
So now I'm taking as many
walks as I can a day.

00:08:58.588 --> 00:09:00.680
(drums beat)

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.192
- I was speaking of billable hours.

00:09:02.192 --> 00:09:05.050
(host laughs)
(Eve laughs)

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.130
- This one I found--

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:07.980
I think this is really funny.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.920
It's just like how the level

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.475
of desperation gets more and more

00:09:11.475 --> 00:09:14.540
as we're getting further into the crisis.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:16.744
- Yeah, and I guess some background here,

00:09:16.744 --> 00:09:20.480
in big firms lawyers get a salary.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.270
But in order to get that salary,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.380
which is generally pretty good

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:24.410
for people that are just coming out

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.600
of law school or college,

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.950
you have to work an
insane amount of hours,

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.335
and those are called billable hours.

00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:31.960
At least in the U.S.,

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.560
the very bottom floor would be probably

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.140
about 2,000 billable hours.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:37.530
And if you're a first- or second-year,

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.440
it's probably more like
2,300 billable hours.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.060
And that's just hours that you can bill

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:44.200
to a client.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.750
It's not the actual hours

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:46.583
that you work in the firm,

00:09:46.583 --> 00:09:48.980
which is often 50% more than that.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:52.100
So you might be working
3,000 or 3,500 hours

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.010
to get 2,000 or 2,300 billable hours.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.790
- I also think it's really interesting,

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.207
and I didn't realize
this before I started,

00:10:00.207 --> 00:10:03.370
how closely you have to record that time.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:04.570
- Oh yeah, right!

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.810
- My time sheets are so detailed,

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:10.630
and I had no idea that that happened.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.100
Also to give context,

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.970
every single task I do per day,

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.830
I have to record by the minute

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.520
because then when the firm wants

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:19.550
to charge a client,

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.930
they then have to turn around and say,

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.940
this is exactly what we spent time on.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.380
So you know exactly what
you're gonna pay for.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.309
But that means that every hour--

00:10:27.309 --> 00:10:29.970
When I first started I
found it really stressful

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:31.010
because I was kind of like,

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.480
do I stop my timers if I go to the toilet?

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.830
It was really intense.

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:37.280
- Yeah, well it depends
on if you're thinking

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.230
about the client in the bathroom or not.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:41.063
- Which I wasn't.

00:10:41.063 --> 00:10:42.223
I did stop my timers.

00:10:43.518 --> 00:10:45.683
(drums beat)

00:10:45.683 --> 00:10:48.470
(host laughs)
(Eve laughs)

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.860
- Keeping up with the
billable requirement here.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.630
Yeah, the bonus in terms

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:55.597
of toilet paper.
- Of toilet paper.

00:10:55.597 --> 00:10:58.072
- I feel like that's an American thing.

00:10:58.072 --> 00:11:00.330
Were there shortages of toilet paper

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:01.460
in Great Britain?

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:02.293
- Oh my God.

00:11:02.293 --> 00:11:03.220
You have no idea.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.220
People went so crazy.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:07.860
We had it here as well,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.790
and the funniest thing is I'd walk past

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:10.913
a supermarket,

00:11:11.912 --> 00:11:13.408
the same supermarket
every day to the firm.

00:11:13.408 --> 00:11:15.600
And every morning there would be lines

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.190
of people holding their toilet paper,

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.230
and it would be sold out

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.724
by 10 minutes past opening.

00:11:20.724 --> 00:11:23.660
I was like, it will get back in stock.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.860
It did not get back in stock U.K.-wide

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:27.920
about five weeks.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.110
I was like, oh my God.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.567
What if I actually run out?

00:11:31.567 --> 00:11:33.510
So unnecessary.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.710
- Yeah, and I don't see why

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.270
the Coronavirus would make people use

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:39.460
more toilet paper.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.410
I guess maybe this is TMI,

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.790
but I've been on team
bidet for a long time,

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.800
so it doesn't really affect me

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:47.633
as much as everybody else.

00:11:47.633 --> 00:11:48.700
- And also I love

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.000
how extreme people were getting.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:50.833
They were like,

00:11:50.833 --> 00:11:52.293
obviously BuzzFeed and companies like

00:11:52.293 --> 00:11:53.987
that were jumping on the bandwagon,

00:11:53.987 --> 00:11:56.194
and like here's how you
can make toilet paper

00:11:56.194 --> 00:11:58.600
out of organic leaves.

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.470
I'm like, I don't wanna ever--

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.890
I don't want it to get to that level.

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:04.420
Can we just not stockpile?

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:06.120
That's an easy solution.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.300
- Yeah, oh my God.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:08.252
Let's not spend too much time

00:12:08.252 --> 00:12:09.270
(Eve laughs)
- On the toilet paper

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:10.547
and move on to the next one here.

00:12:10.547 --> 00:12:12.970
(drums beat)

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:14.640
- Receiving deliveries
during the pandemic,

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:15.536
like leave it on the doorstep

00:12:15.536 --> 00:12:16.706
and get the hell out of here.

00:12:16.706 --> 00:12:18.900
(host laughs)
- I relate to this so hard.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:19.733
But you know what,

00:12:19.733 --> 00:12:21.260
I actually think it's
the other way around.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:22.929
Someone delivered a parcel yesterday,

00:12:22.929 --> 00:12:24.490
and I opened the door,

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.690
and the man he had only just put it down.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:28.183
And he looked up at me,

00:12:28.183 --> 00:12:30.401
and he ran backwards,

00:12:30.401 --> 00:12:32.910
almost fell over his feet.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:33.890
And then he was like,

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:35.830
oh I need to take a photo to prove

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.030
it's been delivered.

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:38.207
So I stood by my parcel,

00:12:38.207 --> 00:12:39.040
and I was like,

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:40.810
I feel now obliged now to pose.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:42.210
So I stood there with my parcel,

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:43.740
like thank you.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.220
He was like, I only needed a picture

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:46.053
of the parcel.

00:12:46.053 --> 00:12:47.118
I didn't need a picture of you.

00:12:47.118 --> 00:12:48.440
(man laughs)
- So now there's

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.640
this weird photo of me and a parcel

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.921
on a database somewhere, like hi.

00:12:52.921 --> 00:12:54.610
- I think that means you're spending

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:55.940
too much time on Instagram.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:57.540
I think you need to step back

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.595
from the influencer
lifestyle just a little bit.

00:12:59.595 --> 00:13:00.428
- Yeah, I know.

00:13:00.428 --> 00:13:01.323
Someone gets out a camera,

00:13:01.323 --> 00:13:03.035
and I'm immediately like, yes!

00:13:03.035 --> 00:13:04.113
Switched on.

00:13:04.113 --> 00:13:05.830
- Skinny arm out in front

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.280
and posing with the parcel.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:09.640
- Yeah, catch my jaw.
- Yeah, I am thanking

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.050
my delivery people like

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:12.982
they are soldiers returning from war.

00:13:12.982 --> 00:13:15.787
They are really making our lives

00:13:15.787 --> 00:13:17.940
I think livable at this point,

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.100
so I'm thankful for--
- Yeah they are incredible.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:21.238
Yeah.

00:13:21.238 --> 00:13:23.428
(drums beat)

00:13:23.428 --> 00:13:24.890
- I don't own anything.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:25.723
Prosecuting attorney,

00:13:25.723 --> 00:13:26.556
will the witness lower his--

00:13:26.556 --> 00:13:29.716
(host laughs)
(Eve laughs)

00:13:29.716 --> 00:13:33.300
- Is Joe Exotic a thing in the U.K.?

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.040
Is Tiger King making rounds around there?

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.690
- So much a thing to the extent

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.220
which three nights ago I was like,

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:40.450
maybe I want a mullet.

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:41.283
And then I was like,

00:13:41.283 --> 00:13:42.116
no I don't.

00:13:42.116 --> 00:13:43.090
No I really don't.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:43.950
- You may not have a choice.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.830
We're all cutting our own hair these days,

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:48.400
so you may end up with one no matter what.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.330
- Yeah, I might slip and then just have

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.330
to become my inner Joe Exotic.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:54.321
All though maybe I'm
more of a Carole Baskin.

00:13:54.321 --> 00:13:55.951
I've not decided yet.

00:13:55.951 --> 00:13:57.970
(host laughs)
Maybe I'm one of the tigers.

00:13:57.970 --> 00:13:58.970
Who knows?

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.930
- Yeah are you team Joe or team Carole?

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.947
- Oh definitely team Carole because--

00:14:02.947 --> 00:14:05.350
and I love how people were kind of like,

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.380
people were kind of like Carole is so,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:08.517
on Twitter people were like,

00:14:08.517 --> 00:14:10.612
Carole is so crazy.

00:14:10.612 --> 00:14:13.470
She potentially murdered her ex-husband

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:14.303
and all this sort of thing.

00:14:14.303 --> 00:14:15.136
And I'm like,

00:14:15.136 --> 00:14:19.129
yeah but he literally keeps 200 tigers

00:14:19.129 --> 00:14:23.280
in his land estate in
the middle of America.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.050
How are we normalizing that already?

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.099
- Well I mean, yeah.
- He's not the normal one

00:14:28.099 --> 00:14:28.932
in this situation.
- Technically Carole

00:14:29.814 --> 00:14:31.380
had lions and tigers as well.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:32.380
But between the two of them,

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.690
one of them is spending two decades

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:35.690
in federal prison,

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.720
and the other is a free person.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:39.920
So, I'm not sure who we should be giving

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.540
the benefit of the doubt to here.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:42.596
- This is true,

00:14:42.596 --> 00:14:44.182
and I actually think about the guy.

00:14:44.182 --> 00:14:47.370
I would love to meet him.
(host laughs)

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.220
Also imagine being his lawyer.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:53.250
I just think that would
be so, so incredible.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:55.080
(drums beat)

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:56.660
All right, working in the office.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:57.830
Working from home.

00:14:57.830 --> 00:14:59.750
Oh, no!
- This speaks

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:00.760
to me on a deep level.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:02.110
I won't lie.
(host laughs)

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:03.700
- So at the moment,

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:05.640
all of our internal meetings

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.490
at the firm are done by camera.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:09.630
Everyone's on camera,

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:10.767
like us on this Zoom.
- Yes.

00:15:10.767 --> 00:15:13.000
- And so, you kind of feel obliged

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.832
to look somewhat not asleep,

00:15:15.832 --> 00:15:17.220
but then equally you're like,

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:18.503
but I'm in my own home.

00:15:18.503 --> 00:15:19.380
It's a very--

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:20.850
at the start some people were,

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.240
everyone was still wearing business wear

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.073
at the start.

00:15:23.073 --> 00:15:23.906
I was like,

00:15:23.906 --> 00:15:24.739
I'm in my own home.

00:15:24.739 --> 00:15:26.100
I'm not putting on a suit.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:27.910
I don't know what's happening.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:30.210
It's regressed and
regressed and regressed.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.009
Now I'm just turning
up in my t-shirt like,

00:15:32.009 --> 00:15:33.666
take me or leave me.

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:35.450
Have me for who I am.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.310
- Yeah, in Florida apparently

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.140
they are having trouble

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:40.760
with the attorneys who are showing up

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:43.500
on the Zoom calls without shirts on.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:44.936
And sometimes you can get away

00:15:44.936 --> 00:15:48.450
with wearing less formal stuff below,

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.250
but I've never heard--

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.620
Well it makes total sense in Florida

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:55.150
that they would show up
in front of the judge

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.230
on a video conference call
with opposing counsel.

00:15:57.230 --> 00:15:58.063
- Oh my gosh.

00:15:58.063 --> 00:15:59.173
In front of a judge?

00:15:59.173 --> 00:16:00.440
- No shirt. No shirt.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.175
- Okay so here's the difference.

00:16:02.175 --> 00:16:05.700
I know in the U.K. that we have now opened

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.790
up virtual courts as well,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:11.380
and I'm 100% sure if I was in any kind

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:14.450
of judicial situation like that I would be

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:17.395
in full, black business wear.

00:16:17.395 --> 00:16:19.140
(drums beat)

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:21.300
- Graduating law school
like I am never going

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:22.270
to financially recover from this.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:24.250
Oh no!

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.350
I had so much law school debt.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.400
I had so much law school debt.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.230
Please tell me that you incurred

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:31.510
a whole bunch of debt as well.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.710
- Okay so, I did,

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:36.989
financial and emotional debt,
(host laughs)

00:16:36.989 --> 00:16:41.670
but I will say I'm pretty
sure law school fees

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:45.176
in America are way higher than the U.K.

00:16:45.176 --> 00:16:46.210
So I kind of don't want
to complain too much

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:47.610
because I'm still in debt,

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:48.443
don't get me wrong,

00:16:48.443 --> 00:16:50.510
but I don't think it's near the level

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:51.360
of the States.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.690
- More or less than the
equivalent of $200,000?

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:56.616
Like more or less than 150,000 pounds?

00:16:56.616 --> 00:16:58.734
(Eve gasps)
- Damn it.

00:16:58.734 --> 00:17:03.120
- I'm at under 25% of that.
(host sighs)

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.670
- For four years, I probably owed

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:09.523
around 30 to 40 thousand pounds.

00:17:09.523 --> 00:17:11.010
- And you're not even gonna be

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.352
a real lawyer doing litigation

00:17:12.352 --> 00:17:13.730
at the end of that.

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:15.270
I feel so bad for you.
- We're gonna fall out.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.640
And I'm ready.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.663
I am ready for us to fall out.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.660
That would be the ultimate head-to-head.

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:22.840
We're gonna come back for round two,

00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:24.000
and I'm gonna have arguments

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.850
as to why transactional lawyers are better

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.125
than litigious lawyers.

00:17:27.125 --> 00:17:28.980
(drums beat)

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.963
- Where can the people
find you on the Internet?

00:17:30.963 --> 00:17:32.270
- You can come along with me

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:33.680
on my YouTube channel.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.407
You can also find me on Instagram

00:17:35.407 --> 00:17:38.150
and Twitter under the
same handles, evecornwell.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:39.790
Why did that feel like an interview?

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:41.280
I'm like--
(Eve laughs)

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:42.830
- Well thank you, Eve, so much,

00:17:43.947 --> 00:17:45.370
and thank you for providing
some human contact

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:47.210
in these really terrible times.

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:48.910
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:51.510
- [Host] Bye, bye.
- Bye!

00:17:53.724 --> 00:17:55.800
- That was fun.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.340
Interacting with people was fun.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:18:00.027
I miss that.
(host sighs)

00:18:00.027 --> 00:18:01.253
Oh well, more memes.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:04.860
(drums beat)

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:06.220
Stop spreading stupid
rumors about martial law.

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:09.000
Completely false, we
will continue closing.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:10.120
This is not martial law.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:12.148
This fall on NBC, Marshall Law.

00:18:12.148 --> 00:18:13.480
(host laughs)

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.090
So this is a pet peeve of mine.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:19.750
It's martial law spelled M-A-R-T-I-A-L,

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.980
as in an army, a martial army.

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:24.210
Not Marshall, like Marshall Mathers.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.530
Though I would totally watch that show,

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:28.680
Marshall Law, and then
you have a crossover

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.250
with Ice-T from Law &amp; Order,

00:18:30.250 --> 00:18:31.486
now that that's been canceled.

00:18:31.486 --> 00:18:33.586
♪ Hi, my name is ♪
- [Announcer] Justice.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:37.210
I mean NBC, make this show.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:38.280
Make Marshall Law.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:39.498
That would be amazing.

00:18:39.498 --> 00:18:41.490
(drums beat)

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.650
Motion to cancel the rest of the year.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.000
Motion granted.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.063
Motion granted.

00:18:47.063 --> 00:18:49.460
Move to strike the previous portion

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:50.540
of this year too.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.800
Man, screw this year.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:54.970
(drums beat)

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:55.803
Dear counsel,

00:18:55.803 --> 00:18:56.760
by opening up this email you have agreed

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:58.000
to accept electronic service

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:59.150
and stipulated that our motion

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:00.600
for summary judgment will be unopposed.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.390
Stay safe.
(host laughs)

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:03.550
So this is another thing

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.240
that lawyers have to deal with.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:07.560
Except under these
extenuating circumstances,

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:09.760
you have to physically
mail almost everything

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:12.640
or personally deliver motions,

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:14.120
or discovery requests,

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:15.560
and you have to physically file the stuff

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:16.393
with the court.

00:19:16.393 --> 00:19:17.250
I've never understood,

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.330
except for sort of traditional reasons,

00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:20.510
why this is.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:22.820
Now that everyone can't
really go to court,

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:23.770
